The balance of the moment of momentum about a fixed point $\vec{x}_0$ for a moving control volume $V_c(t)$ is given by:

$$ \begin{aligned}
\mathbf{\frac{d}{dt}\left[\int_{V_c(t)} \rho[(\vec{x}-\vec{x}_0)\wedge\vec{v}] \, dV\right] + \int_{\Sigma_c(t)} \rho[(\vec{x}-\vec{x}_0)\wedge\vec{v}][(\vec{v}-\vec{v}_c)\cdot\vec{n}] \, d\sigma =} \\
\mathbf{-\int_{\Sigma_c(t)} (\vec{x}-\vec{x}_0)\wedge(p\vec{n}) \, d\sigma + \int_{\Sigma_c(t)} (\vec{x}-\vec{x}_0)\wedge(\bar{\bar{\tau}}'\cdot\vec{n}) \, d\sigma + \int_{V_c(t)} \rho[(\vec{x}-\vec{x}_0)\wedge\vec{f}_m] \, dV}
\end{aligned} \qquad \text{[Eq. 3.38]} $$

Applying the Reynolds Transport Theorem (Eq. 3.8):

$$ \begin{aligned}
\mathbf{\frac{d}{dt}\left[\int_{V_c(t)} \rho\left(e + \frac{|\vec{v}|^2}{2}\right) dV\right] + \int_{\Sigma_c(t)} \rho\left(e + \frac{|\vec{v}|^2}{2}\right)[(\vec{v}-\vec{v}_c)\cdot\vec{n}] \, d\sigma =} \\
\mathbf{-\int_{\Sigma_c(t)} p\vec{v}\cdot\vec{n} \, d\sigma + \int_{\Sigma_c(t)} \vec{v}\cdot\bar{\bar{\tau}}'\cdot\vec{n} \, d\sigma + \int_{V_c(t)} \rho\vec{f}_m\cdot\vec{v} \, dV} \\
\mathbf{-\int_{\Sigma_c(t)} \vec{q}\cdot\vec{n} \, d\sigma + \int_{V_c(t)} (Q_c + Q_r) \, dV}
\end{aligned} \qquad \text{[Eq. 3.46]} $$

The $\theta$ component of (4.41), steady, with $\partial/\partial\theta = 0$ and no mass force:

$$ \rho\left(v_r\frac{\partial v_\theta}{\partial r} + \frac{v_rv_\theta}{r} + v_z\frac{\partial v_\theta}{\partial z}\right) = \mu\left[\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(\frac{1}{r}\frac{\partial(rv_\theta)}{\partial r}\right) + \frac{\partial^2v_\theta}{\partial z^2}\right] \tag{3} $$

Each term for $v_\theta = r\Omega g$, $v_r = r\Omega f'$, $v_z = -2(\nu\Omega)^{1/2}f$ (the $\eta$ derivatives bring $(\Omega/\nu)^{1/2}$):

- $v_r\,\partial v_\theta/\partial r = (r\Omega f')(\Omega g) = r\Omega^2f'g$ and $v_rv_\theta/r = (r\Omega f')(\Omega g) = r\Omega^2f'g$;
- $v_z\,\partial v_\theta/\partial z = -2(\nu\Omega)^{1/2}f\cdot r\Omega g'\,(\Omega/\nu)^{1/2} = -2r\Omega^2fg'$;
- the first viscous term is zero: $r^{-1}\partial(rv_\theta)/\partial r = r^{-1}\partial(r^2\Omega g)/\partial r = 2\Omega g$ does not depend on $r$;
- $\partial^2v_\theta/\partial z^2 = r\Omega g''\,\Omega/\nu$.

Substituting and using $\mu/\nu = \rho$:

$$ \rho\,r\Omega^2\left(2f'g - 2fg'\right) = \rho\,r\Omega^2g'' \;\Longrightarrow\; \boxed{\,g'' + 2\left(f\,g' - f'g\right) = 0\,} \tag{4} $$

